Rest operator:

- ES2015 introduces a new syntax to define variable parameters in functions. As said in the previous
    section, you could always pass extra arguments to a function and get them with the special
    arguments variable. So you could have done something like this:

            function addPonies(ponies) {
                for (var i = 0; i < arguments.length; i++) {
                    poniesInRace.push(arguments[i]);
                }
            }
            addPonies('Rainbow Dash', 'Pinkie Pie');
        
    -> But we can agree that it’s neither pretty nor obvious: since the ponies parameter is never
        used, how do we know that we can pass several ponies?
    -> ES2015 gives us a way better syntax, using the rest operator …:

            function addPonies(...ponies) {
                for (let pony of ponies) {
                    poniesInRace.push(pony);
                }
            }
    
    -> ponies is now a true array on which we can iterate.
    -> The for … of loop used for iteration is also a new feature in ES2015. It makes sure that you
        iterate over the collection values, and not also over its properties as for … in would do.

- The rest operator can also work when destructuring data:

            const [winner, ...losers] = poniesInRace;
    
    -> assuming 'poniesInRace' is an array containing several ponies 'winner' will have the first
        pony, and 'losers' will be an array of the other ones


⚠️ THE REST OPERATOR IS NOT TO BE CONFUSED WITH THE SPREAD OPERATOR WHICH LOOKS AWFULLY SIMILAR!
    BUT THE SPREAD OPERATOR IS THE OPPOSITE: IT TAKES AN ARRAY AND SPREADS IT IN VARIABLE
    ARGUMENTS. 
    (Spread operator)

            const category = 'Computing';
            const categories = ['Gaming', 'Multimedia'];
            const productCategories = [...categories, category]; 

    -> const productCategories = ['Gaming', 'Multimedia', 'Computing']
    -> The current behavior: NOT CHANGING A VARIABLE, INSTEAD CREATE A NEW VERSION THAT COMES FROM
        THE ORIGINAL IS CALLED IMMUTABILITY(once a value is created, it is not changed. If you want
        a different value, you create a new one)
    
    (Spread operator on objects)

            const product = {
                name: 'Keyboard',
                price: 75
            };
            const newProduct = {
                ...product,
                price: 100,
                category: 'Computing'
            };

    ->  const newProduct = {
            name: 'Keyboard',
            price: 100,
            category: 'Computing'
        }
    -> The newProduct object takes the properties from the product object, adds new values on top
        of it, and replaces the existing ones. That's why 'price: 100'.


⚠️ (For...of vs For...in)

            const expenses = [100, 200, 300];

    1) For...in:

            for (let e in expenses) {
                console.log(e);
            }
        -> Iterates over Indexes/Keys, not values.
        -> best used for objects
        -> Output:  0
                    1
                    2
    
    2) For...of:

            for (let e of expenses) {
                console.log(e);
            }
        
        -> Iterates over values.
        -> best used for arrays and iterables
        -> Output:  100
                    200
                    300
